{
  "abstract": "Lean 4 (v4.35.0) + Mathlib formalization of the base-b last-digit Collatz family f_b(n) = n/2 (even n) and (n mod b)*n + 1 (odd n), focusing on the base-10 Ouroboros map f = f_10 and stating the open OuroborosConjecture definition alongside 38 unconditionally proved compared theorems (zero sorry, zero native_decide, standard axioms only). Collectively, the 38 theorems establish six principal result families: (1) small-base resolutions, including base-4 equivalence to the classical 3x+1 Collatz conjecture and global convergence to 1 in bases 2 and 5; (2) base-10 exponential Terras stopping-time descent (p_k <= 1.124953 * 0.996423^k) and density-one descent, together with a complete all-even-base stopping-time dichotomy (p_infty(b) = 0 iff b in {2, 4, 6, 10}) and even-base drift classification; (3) the base-10 basin-of-1 modulo-30 residue law, exact 2-adic Cantor closure and ultrametric isometry, and exact Hausdorff and box-counting dimension dim_H = dim_B = beta = -log_2 a (plus congruence-class completions and exact base-b suffix-closure dimension in every base b >= 2); (4) sharp pure-kernel basin growth bounds (0.0145 X^(399/800) <= N_1(X) <= C X^0.6245 with limsup (log N_1 / log X) <= 0.6245 < beta, N_5(X) > 0.02134 X^(13/20), and strict separation N_1(X)/N_5(X) <= C X^(-0.0255) -> 0); (5) universal odd-base Markov absorption for all odd b >= 3 and a certified 905/999 odd-base survey through b <= 1999 (27 trap-only and 878 split rational densities, plus explicit b = 3, 5, 7, 13 evaluations); and (6) base-10 cycle obstructions (digit-cancellation, halving-budget, K > 64000 tail, and unconditional classification of all cycles with m <= 3 odd members alongside the two known cycles through 1 and 5). Main results in the companion paper: 15 of 16 proved main results in Lean 4 (94%) · 1 of 16 by external computation (exhaustive sweep and cycle lower bounds through 10^15) · 0 by written proof · Global conjecture open. Within this package, 19 library modules in Ouroboros/ (plus Challenge.lean and Solution.lean) import Mathlib directly while the 104 finite certificate shards (BasinFiveCert*, EvenBasesCert, BasinOneLowerCert*, BasinOneUpperCert*, and OddSurveyCert*) verify finite integer inequalities by Init-only kernel reduction (rfl). Companion working paper, exact external checkers, and logged prior-work searches are published at https://mathdiscoveries.org/results/the-ouroboros-conjecture.html.",
